('alict_02135', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'The ages of a father and a son are 2C16 and 10002 respectively. In how many years will the father be 4 times as old as his jhi 2C16 10002 mshdf.a jhi Tyqf.a fuka 4 jkq o@ Answer : 4) Let''s first convert the given values from their respective bases into decimal wdldrfhka lruq'' The father''s age is given as 2C16, and the son''s age is given as 10002. mshdf.a jhi 2C16 jhi 10002 olajd the father''s age is 44 years and the son''s age is 8 years. tuksid" mshdf.a jhi 44 jhi 8 x years', '["1", "2", "3", "4", "5 2025/07/04 Information and Communication Technology"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Correct answer: "1". This follows from converting/calculating the given number(s) using standard place-value (base) arithmetic.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2135, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02136', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'Which of the following is the result of the 2''s complement addition of the following 8-bit binary numbers?', '["00010100", "00010011", "100010100", "100010011", "11100110"]'::jsonb, 1, '• Step 1 = Convert -25 to its 8-bit 2''s complement form -25 tys 2 To find the 2''s complement of a binary first invert all the bits (change 0 to 1 and 1 to 0). Then add 1 to the inverted bits. This gives the binary representation of the negative number', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2136, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02137', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'If A = 65 in what word is shown by the following decimal sequence?', '["MANGO", "PARTY", "PAINT", "PANDA", "BLACK"]'::jsonb, 1, 'ASCII (American Standard Code for Information Interchange) assigns a unique decimal number to each including and symbols. It''s widely used in programming and data representation. For uppercase letters range from 65 (A) to 90 (Z)', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2137, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02138', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'What fundamental difference distinguishes Unicode from ASCII in terms of character encoding?', '["Unicode uses variable-length allowing it to represent a wider range of characters compared to the fixed-length encoding of ASCII", "Unicode encodes characters using a single while ASCII requires multiple bytes for character representation", "Unicode is primarily used for encoding English whereas ASCII is designed for encoding characters in non-Latin scripts", "ASCII is a superset of encompassing all characters represented in the Unicode standard. ASCII", "Unicode was developed as an extension of incorporating additional control characters for enhanced text processing"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Variable-Length Encoding vs. Fixed-Length Encoding Unicode Uses variable-length encoding. This means that characters can be represented using different numbers of bytes (e.g., 1, 2, 3, or 4 bytes)', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2138, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02139', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'A = 0.7510 X = Binary Value of A ( A w.h ) Y = Octal Value of A ( A w.h ) Which of the following answers represents the values of X and Y X Y lrkafka 3) Explanation', '["112 , 0.608", "0.012 , 0.78", "0.112 , 0.68", "102 , 6.08", "0.112 , 6.08 3"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Correct answer: "112 , 0.608". This follows from converting/calculating the given number(s) using standard place-value (base) arithmetic.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2139, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02140', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'What is the result of adding -35 to 78 using 8-bit Two''s Complement representation?', '["00100000\u2082", "11111010\u2082", "001010112", "11001001\u2082", "10011111\u2082"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Correct answer: "001010112". This follows from converting/calculating the given number(s) using standard place-value (base) arithmetic.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2140, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02141', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'What is the result of the following expression in decimal?', '["750", "820", "880", "910", "860"]'::jsonb, 5, 'Correct answer: "860". This follows from converting/calculating the given number(s) using standard place-value (base) arithmetic.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2141, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02142', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'A system uses a base -16 (hexadecimal) representation for storing RGB color values. If the color is represented as what is the decimal value of the green RGB j¾K 16 mdofha ksfhdackhla lrhs'' #98EF1B j¾Kh lrkafka ixrplfha w.h 5) Explanation', '["152", "27", "254", "357", "EF16 E = 14 F = 15 161 160 16 x 14 1 x 15 224+15 23910 4. Calculate the two''s complement of the number 110111012 represented by signed magnitude"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The leftmost bit of a binary number represented by signed magnitude is 1 if it is a negative number and 0 if it is a positive number. 0 the number 110111012 here is the number -010111012. fuys Next the first complement of -010111012 should be obtained', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2142, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02143', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'What is the SOP expression that can be derived from the Karnaugh map below?', '["ASCII", "Unicode", "ISO-8859-1", "ANSI", "EBCDIC"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Correct answer: "ASCII". This follows from converting/calculating the given number(s) using standard place-value (base) arithmetic.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2143, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02144', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', '110010102 + 000011112 - 101011102 What is the two''s complement of the number obtained from the above .Kkh ,efnk foflys 1) Explanation', '["001010112", "110101002", "110101012", "001011002", "111011002 This is a positive number. The two''s complement of a positive number is the binary value of that number. Ok"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Correct answer: "001010112". This follows from converting/calculating the given number(s) using standard place-value (base) arithmetic.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2144, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02145', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'What is the two''s complement of -11? -11 foflys 3) Explanation', '["00001011", "11110100", "11110101", "11111101", "00001100 5"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Correct answer: "00001011". This follows from converting/calculating the given number(s) using standard place-value (base) arithmetic.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2145, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02146', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'Which of the following circuits is used as a "Memory device" in Wmdx.hla" lrkafka ljrla 2) Let''s consider each option one by one', '["Rectifier This statement is incorrect. A rectifier converts alternating current (AC) to direct current (DC) and is not used for memory storage in computers. iDcqldrlhla (AC)", "Flip Flop This statement is correct. A flip -flop is a basic memory device in often used in digital electronics to store binary data (0 or", "It can maintain a stable state and is a fundamental building block of memory circuits. flip-flop", "Comparator This statement is incorrect. A comparator is a device that compares two voltages or currents and outputs a binary signal based on the comparison. It is not used for memory storage. comparator", "Attenuator This statement is incorrect. An attenuator reduces the power of a signal without significantly distorting its waveform. It is used for signal not memory storage. attenuator tys"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Correct answer: "Flip Flop This statement is correct. A flip -flop is a basic memory device in often used in digital electronics to store binary data (0 or". This follows from converting/calculating the given number(s) using standard place-value (base) arithmetic.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2146, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02147', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'X = 2''s complement of 120 Y = 1768 Select the answer that contains the values obtained when X and Y are subjected to bitwise AND XOR operations respectively. X Y', '["011110002, 000001102", "111111102, 011111102", "000010002, 111101112", "111111102, 111101102", "111111102, 000010012"]'::jsonb, 1, 'IBM 2) Intel 3) AMD 4) Honeywell 5) Apple Y - Bitwise XOR 0 1 0 1 0 1 0 Bitwise AND 0 1 0 1 0 1 0 the corresponding binary values for X and Y should be obtained', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2147, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02148', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'What is the primary difference between a half adder and a full adder?', '["A half adder can add more than two binary numbers simultaneously", "A half adder considers carry from previous additions. w\u00beO fmr", "A full adder can handle carry input from previous additions. mQ\u00beK fmr", "A full adder outputs a binary number in two''s complement form", "A half adder has more logic gates than a full adder"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Correct answer: "A full adder can handle carry input from previous additions. mQ¾K fmr". This follows from converting/calculating the given number(s) using standard place-value (base) arithmetic.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2148, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02149', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'Which of the following answers is not a valid Binary Coded Decimal (BCD) value?', '["10001000 BCD This value can be split into two groups 1000 and 1000, which represent decimal digits 8 and 8. Both are valid BCD digits. w.h", "11110001 BCD When split into two groups 1111 and 0001, 1111 represents a binary value of 15, which is not a valid BCD digit. This makes this value an invalid BCD", "10010111 BCD Split into 1001 and 0111, which represent decimal digits 9 and 7. Both are valid BCD digits", "00010101 BCD Split into 0001 and 0101, which represent decimal digits 1 and 5. Both are valid BCD digits", "00110011 BCD Split into 0011 and 0011, which represent decimal digits 3 and 3. Both are valid BCD digits"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Correct answer: "10010111 BCD Split into 1001 and 0111, which represent decimal digits 9 and 7. Both are valid BCD digits". This follows from converting/calculating the given number(s) using standard place-value (base) arithmetic.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2149, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02150', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'In character which standard can represent the widest range of characters?', '["ASCII This option is incorrect. ASCII (American Standard Code for Information Interchange) can only represent 128 which is a limited set", "EBCDIC This option is incorrect. EBCDIC (Extended Binary Coded Decimal Interchange Code) can only represent 256 characters and mostly used for specific older systems. It''s an eight -bit character encoding system primarily used on IBM mainframe and midrange computer operating systems", "Unicode This option is correct. Unicode can represent a vast number of characters from almost all writing systems in the making it the most extensive character encoding standard", "BCD This option is incorrect. BCD (Binary -Coded Decimal) is not used for character encoding but rather for representing decimal digits in binary form", "Gray code This option is incorrect. Gray code is used for error detection and correction in digital systems"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Correct answer: "Unicode This option is correct. Unicode can represent a vast number of characters from almost all writing systems in the making it the most extensive character encoding standard". This follows from converting/calculating the given number(s) using standard place-value (base) arithmetic.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2150, 'A/L Past Paper Series')
INSERT INTO public.questions (id, unit, unit_title, question_text, options, correct_option, explanation, latex_formula, trap_insight, difficulty, topic, past_paper_year, past_paper_number, series)
VALUES
('alict_02151', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'Which of the following statements regarding number system conversions is', '["ASCII", "EBCDIC", "Unicode", "BCD", "Gray code"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Correct answer: "EBCDIC". This follows from converting/calculating the given number(s) using standard place-value (base) arithmetic.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2151, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02152', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'Calculate the sum of +45 and -25 using 8-bit 1''s complement representation. What is the final result in binary?', '["00010100", "11101011", "01000110", "10111001", "00010011"]'::jsonb, 1, '• Step 1 = Represent +45 and -25 in binary 2 45 2 22 - 1 2 11 - 0 2 5 - 1 2 - 1 2 1 - 0 - 1 • Step 2 = Convert to 1st complement In 1''s complement only negative numbers are converted into their complement form. Positive numbers remain in their regular binary format', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2152, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02153', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'What is the hexadecimal equivalent of the octal number 2364.56₈?', '["D4.C3\u2081\u2086", "F4.B8\u2081\u2086", "E7.A2\u2081\u2086", "A1.9F\u2081\u2086", "F3.C1\u2081\u2086"]'::jsonb, 2, 'We start by converting each digit of the octal number to a 3-digit binary number. Octal digits go from 0 to 7, and each can be written in 3 bits. So we take the number 2364.56₈ and break it down. wms Base 8 2364.568 2 3 6 4 5 6 010 011 110 100 101 110 Base 2 010011110100', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2153, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02154', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'The IEEE 754 32-bit representation of the decimal number -6.5 has which of the following as its sign bit and exponent?', '["Sign = 1, Exponent = 10000001", "Sign = 0, Exponent = 10000001", "Sign = 1, Exponent = 10000010", "Sign = 1, Exponent = 01111110", "Sign = 0, Exponent = 10000010 Answer 1"]'::jsonb, 1, 'IEEE 754 is a standard way to store decimal numbers in binary. In the 32-bit the number is split into three parts. IEEE 754 fldgia Part Size (bits) Sign 1 Exponent 8 Mantissa 23 The sign bit simply indicates whether the number is positive or negative', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2154, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02155', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'What happens when you add two numbers in two''s complement representation and the result produces a carry-out of the most significant bit?', '["The carry bit affects the sum", "The carry bit is added back to the sum", "The carry bit is inverted", "The carry bit is stored separately", "The carry bit is discarded"]'::jsonb, 5, 'Correct answer: "The carry bit is discarded". This follows from converting/calculating the given number(s) using standard place-value (base) arithmetic.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2155, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02156', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'In character which of the following is a 7-bit standard?', '["Unicode", "ASCII", "EBCDIC", "UTF-8", "Gray code"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Unicode This is a much larger standard designed to handle all the world''s languages. It is a super-set of but is not limited to 7 bits. tlla ASCII This is the original and most common 7-bit character encoding standard', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2156, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02157', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'Evaluate the following expression and give the result in decimal (base 10)', '["32.625", "33.75", "34.25", "35.5", "30.5"]'::jsonb, 1, 'We start by converting each number from its base into decimal wms 1101.1012 8 4 2 1 .5 .25 .125 Base 2 1 0 1 0 1 8 4 0 1 .5 0 .125 Base 10', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2157, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02158', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'In 2''s complement binary how is the carry bit handled? 2', '["It is added back into the sum", "It is stored separately for later use", "It is discarded", "It is inverted before being added to the sum", "It affects the final sum"]'::jsonb, 3, 'In two''s complement the carry-out from the most significant bit (the leftmost bit) is simply ignored. fofla wxl fjfiis tl& The result of the addition is determined by the bits that fit within the fixed-width representation', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2158, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02159', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'What is the decimal equivalent of the 2''s complement of binary 11100110₂?', '["-26", "-25", "-22", "-27", "-30"]'::jsonb, 1, 'To find the magnitude of the take the 2''s complement of the binary value. 1 0 1 0 1 0 1 +1 0 1 0 1 0 27 26 25 24 23 22 21 20 128 64 32 16 8 4 2 1 16 + 8 + 2 = 26 Since the most significant bit (the leftmost bit) is a 1, the number is negative', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2159, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02160', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'Which of the following encoding schemes can represent emojis and characters in every known language?', '["ASCII", "EBCDIC", "ANSI", "Unicode", "UTF-7"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Unicode Universal character encoding standard designed to be a superset of all other character encoding schemes. It provides a unique number (a code point) for every regardless of the or language', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2160, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02161', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'The hexadecimal value 4A2 represents a certain decimal value. What is the equivalent binary representation of this decimal value?', '["010010100010", "10010100010", "0100101000100000", "1001010001000000", "111111010011"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Correct answer: "10010100010". This follows from converting/calculating the given number(s) using standard place-value (base) arithmetic.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2161, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02162', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', '1210, 218, 1616, 110112, ... The next term of this number pattern', '["30", "31", "32", "33", "Converting 1616 into Decimal 1616"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Correct answer: "30". This follows from converting/calculating the given number(s) using standard place-value (base) arithmetic.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2162, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02163', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'In the above Python function decimal_to_binary(num), what does the conditional statement if num < 0 or num > 15: ensure?', '["It checks if the input number is a prime number. th oehs", "It converts any negative number to a positive number. th Ok", "It restricts the input number to a range between 0 and 15 for valid binary conversion. th", "It ensures that the input number is less than or equal to 15. th 15 g wvq iudk nj", "It verifies if num is an even number. tu oehs"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The conditional statement if num ensures that the input number is within the range of 0 to 15. This restriction is important because binary conversion for this function is designed to handle numbers within this which can be represented with up to 4 binary digits', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2163, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02164', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'Find the 2''s complement of -9910 using 8 8la -9910 2 1) Explanation', '["10011101", "10011100", "11100100", "11100000", "11111110 4. 000001012 - 000000112 = .................... The answer to the above blank A"]'::jsonb, 3, 'the correct answer is 3). 5', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2164, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02165', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'Which of the following will give an error when casting? Cast ,nd fokafka ljrla 1) Explanation of each option', '["int(''1.2'') This will raise a because ''1.2'' is a string representation of a floating-point and int() cannot directly convert floating-point strings that are not whole numbers. ''1.2''", "bool(''0.0'') This will not give an error. In any non-empty string evaluates to True when converted to Boolean. ''0.0'' will be converted to True. ,nd fkdfokq", "bin(128) This will not give an error. bin() converts an integer to its binary representation as a string prefixed with ''0b''. bin(128) will give ''0b10000000''. ,nd fkdfokq", "float(12) This will not give an error. float() can convert integers to floating-point numbers. float(12) will give 12.0. ,nd fkdfokq", "str(21.5) This will not give an error. str() converts a value to a string representation. str(21.5) will give ''21.5''. ,nd fkdfokq"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Python range() function returns a sequence of in a given range. The most common use of it is to iterate sequences on a sequence of numbers using Python loops', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2165, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02166', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'Which of the following will not give a False output in False ,nd fkdfokafka ljrla 2) Explanation', '["bool(0) The integer 0 is considered False in Python. bool(0) evaluates to False. 0", "bool(''0'') A non-empty string in Python is considered even if it contains just the character 0. bool(''0'') evaluates to True. Python ysia string tlla True", "bool(0.0) The floating-point number 0.0 is considered False in Python. bool(0.0) evaluates to False. Python 0.0 floating-point wxlhla False", "bool(0x0000) The hexadecimal 0x0000 is equivalent to the integer 0, which is considered False. bool(0x0000) evaluates to False. 0x0000 0 g iudk", "bool(()) An empty tuple is considered False in Python. bool(()) evaluates to False. Python ysia tuple tlla False"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Correct answer: "bool(''0'') A non-empty string in Python is considered even if it contains just the character 0. bool(''0'') evaluates to True. Python ysia string tlla True". This follows from converting/calculating the given number(s) using standard place-value (base) arithmetic.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2166, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02167', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'Given the binary ,nd the statements m%ldY The decimal equivalent of the number is 182', '["A only. A", "B only. B", "A and B only. A B", "B and C only. B C", "All of the above"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Let''s first get the decimal equivalent of the given binary number. 101101102 1 0 1 0 1 0 27 26 25 24 23 22 21 20 128 0 32 16 0 4 2 0 128 + 32 + 16 + 4 + 2 18210 the statement A is correct the Statement C is proven wrong as the decimal equivalent is 182 not 186', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2167, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02168', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'A = 7416 B = 23210 C = 1648 Which of the above is equivalent to binary ioyka 011101002 g iudk fudkjdo? Answer : 4) Explanation', '["A only. A", "B only. B", "C only. C", "A and C only. A C", "None of the above"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Correct answer: "A only. A". This follows from converting/calculating the given number(s) using standard place-value (base) arithmetic.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2168, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02169', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'Which of the following is the correct decimal value of the 8-bit two''s complement binary number?', '["\u221210", "\u22126", "\u22128", "\u221214", "\u22124"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Let''s decode the 8-bit two''s complement fofla 8l Given binary Step In two''s the first bit (MSB) indicates the sign. fofla 1 → negative number Step 2 - Find magnitude For negative invert all bits and add 1', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2169, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02170', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'Given the 4-bit binary number 1101, the output of a circuit that takes each bit as input into the logic 4-bit', '["0", "1", "10", "11", "undefined"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The input is a 4-bit binary number 1 0 1 MSB LSB A B C D Octal to decimal 2618 2 6 1 82 81 80 64×2 8×6 1×1 = 17710 1) Hexa-decimal to decimal BC16 B C 161 160 16×11 1×12 = 18810 Binary to decimal 101010112 1 0 1 0 1 0 1 128 0 3', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2170, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02171', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'If the ASCII value of the letter "A" is "65" then how is "APSARA" represented in ASCII?', '["65 79 82 65 83 65", "65 80 83 60 82 65", "65 80 83 65 82 60", "65 69 82 65 81 65", "65 80 83 65 82 65"]'::jsonb, 5, 'Correct answer: "65 80 83 65 82 65". This follows from converting/calculating the given number(s) using standard place-value (base) arithmetic.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2171, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02172', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'Convert 15210 into BCD. 15210 , BCD', '["000101101000BCD", "000010010111BCD", "000101010010BCD", "010001101110BCD", "100110010010BCD"]'::jsonb, 3, 'BCD takes 4 bits to represent one character. So here each digit should be written by 4 bits. BCD u.ska 4la u.ska So the answer is 3. 4', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2172, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02173', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', '11101102 - ............. = 73 What is the correct answer that fits the blank here? fuys 2) Explanation', '["48", "D16", "55", "608", "1010012 X 0 1 0 1 46 0 1 0 1 0 Y 0 1 0 1 AND gate A B F 0 1 0 1 0 1 XOR gate A B F 0 1 0 1 0 Bitwise AND X 0 1 0 1 Y 0 1 0 1 0 1 0 1 Bitwise XOR X 0 1 0 1 Y 0 1 0 1 0 1 0 1 0 Then the appropriate value for the blank is decimal 45. miqj"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Correct answer: "D16". This follows from converting/calculating the given number(s) using standard place-value (base) arithmetic.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2173, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02174', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'Convert the hexadecimal number 2F.A₁₆ into and decimal. Which of the following is correct?', '["101111.101\u2082, 57.5\u2088, 47.625\u2081\u2080", "101111.101\u2082, 57.46\u2088, 47.625\u2081\u2080", "101111.101\u2082, 57.52\u2088, 47.65\u2081\u2080", "101111.110\u2082, 57.5\u2088, 47.75\u2081\u2080", "101111.101\u2082, 57.5\u2088, 46.625\u2081\u2080"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Hexadecimal to Binary 2F.A 2 F A Binary 0 1 0 1 0 1 0 101111.10102 2.Binary to Octal Binary 1 0 1 0 1 0 Octal 5 7 5 0 57.5 8 3.Binary to Decimal Binary 1 0 1 0 1 0 Decimal 32 16 8 4 2 1 0.5 0.25 0.125 0.0625 32+8+4+2+1+0.5+0.125 = 47.625', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2174, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02175', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'Which of the following statements about Binary-Coded Decimal (BCD) is true?', '["BCD is more efficient in terms of storage compared to pure binary representation", "Each decimal digit in BCD is represented by a 3-bit binary code. BCD", "The BCD representation of the decimal number 25 is 0010 0101. wxl 25 BCD 0010 0101", "BCD is primarily used for complex scientific calculations. BCD m%Odk", "All possible 4-bit binary combinations are valid in the BCD system. BCD"]'::jsonb, 3, 'In each decimal digit is converted to its 4-bit binary equivalent. 2 is represented as 0010, and 5 is represented as 0101. Combining them gives 0010 0101. BCD ixLHdxlhlau tys Option 1) BCD requires more bits to represent the same decimal number compared to pure binary', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2175, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02176', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'Which of the following statements about a Full Adder is correct?', '["A Full Adder can add only two single-bit binary numbers", "A Full Adder produces two Sum and Carry. mQ\u00beK folla ksmojhs(", "A Full Adder requires only one Half Adder to be implemented", "A Full Adder does not use any logic gates", "A Full Adder cannot be cascaded to add multi-bit binary numbers"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The Full Adder always produces two Sum = A ⊕ B ⊕ Cin Carry = (A·B) + (B·Cin) + (A·Cin) It calculates the sum of the three input bits and a carry-out to the next stage. th Option 1) A Full Adder adds three single-bit binary numbers (two input bits and a carry-in bit)', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2176, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02177', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'In a security alarm system implemented using an SR which of the following statements is correct?', '["The Set (S) input activates the and the Reset (R) input deactivates it. Set (S)", "The SR Latch can only store binary 0, not 1. SR Latch yg", "If both S and R are 1 the alarm enters a stable state. S R folu 1", "SR Latches cannot be used in security alarm systems", "The output of the SR Latch changes randomly regardless of inputs"]'::jsonb, 1, 'S = 1, R = 0 Activates (sets) the alarm. S = 0, R = 1 Deactivates (resets) the alarm. S = R = 0 Maintains the previous state. fmr S = R = 1 Considered invalid', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2177, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02178', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'The 8-bit two''s complement number 11101010₂ is given. What is its equivalent decimal value in the original signed form?', '["-22", "-21", "-54", "-86", "+234"]'::jsonb, 1, '• Check sign bit (MSB) The number is 11101010₂. Since the MSB = 1, it''s a negative number in two''s complement form. MSB = 1 • Find the magnitude (original binary) To get the original number from two''s invert the bits and add 1', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2178, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02179', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'Given the binary number', '["18710, BB16", "18910, BC16", "18310, B716", "19110, BF16", "17510, AF16"]'::jsonb, 1, '101110112 in decimal 1 0 1 0 1 27 26 25 24 23 22 21 20 128 0 32 16 8 0 2 1 128 + 32 + 16 + 8 + 2 + 1 18710 101110112 in hexadecimal 1 0 1 0 1 23 22 21 20 23 22 21 20 8 0 2 1 8 0 2 1 8 + 2 + 1 = B 8 + 2 + 1 = B BB16', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2179, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02180', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'Given two 8-bit unsigned binary numbers', '["000100102", "001011102", "010110102", "100100102", "110010002"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Step-by-Step explanation Convert A and B to decimal A = 001011012 0 1 0 1 0 1 27 26 25 24 23 22 21 20 0 32 0 8 4 0 1 32 + 8 + 4 + 1 4510 B = 000110112 0 1 0 1 27 26 25 24 23 22 21 20 0 16 8 0 2 1 16 + 8 + 2 + 1 2710 45 = 27 = 18 in binary 2 18 2 9 -0 2 4 -1 2 -0 1 -0 18 in 8-b', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2180, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02181', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'Calculate the two''s complement of the number 110111012 represented by signed magnitude', '["152", "27", "254", "357", "239"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Correct answer: "152". This follows from converting/calculating the given number(s) using standard place-value (base) arithmetic.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2181, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02182', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'What is the purpose of ASCII code?', '["To compress images", "To represent characters using numbers", "To encrypt data", "To format hard drives", "To block viruses"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The purpose of ASCII (American Standard Code for Information Interchange) is to represent such as and using numerical values. Each character is assigned a unique numeric code within a range of 0 to 127 in the standard ASCII set', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2182, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02183', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'The decimal transition corresponding to BA5 hexadecimal transition and the 8-bit two''s complement representation corresponding to -17 are BA5 -17 g', '["2881, 101011112", "2891, 111011012", "2981, 111011112", "2981, 111011102", "2981, 111101112"]'::jsonb, 3, 'BA516 B A (10) 5 162 161 160 256 × 11 = 2816 16 × 10 = 160 1 × 5 = 5 2816 + 160 + 5 = 2981 298110 BA516 = 298110 let''s find the 8-bit two''s complement of -17', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2183, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02184', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'Convert the binary number 1011.1012 into its and octal equivalents. Which of the following options correctly represents all three conversions?', '["1011.1012 = 11.75 = B.C16 = 13.68", "1011.1012 = 11.625 = B.A16 = 13.58", "1011.1012 = 10.625 = A.A16 = 12.58", "1011.1012 = 11.375 = B.616 = 13.38", "1011.1012 = 11.625 = B.916 = 13.78"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Step 1: Binary to Decimal Conversion 1( Binary 1 0 1 0 1 Decimal 8 4 2 1 0.5 0.25 0.125 8+2+1+0.5+0.125 = 11.625 Step 2 :Binary to Hexadecimal Conversion 2( Binary 1011.1012 1011 1010 Hexa 8 4 2 1 8 4 2 1 8+2+1 8+2 = A B', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2184, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02185', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'In an 8-bit 2''s complement what is the range of decimal values that can be represented?', '["-128 to +128", "-127 to +127", "-128 to +127", "-127 to +128", "-256 to +255"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Correct answer: "-128 to +127". This follows from converting/calculating the given number(s) using standard place-value (base) arithmetic.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2185, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02186', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'What is the decimal value of the answer obtained after performing the bitwise XOR operation on 11011011 and 10111010?', '["1100001", "96", "405", "97", "251"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Correct answer: "97". This follows from converting/calculating the given number(s) using standard place-value (base) arithmetic.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2186, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
