('alict_02197', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'The number 111011102 is expressed in signed magnitude representation. Two''s complement of that number is equal hk olajd tu foflys wkqmQrlhg iudk jkafka" A. 100100102 B. 2228 C. 9216 Answer : 4) Let''s find the two''s complement of the binary number wxl 111011102 foflys fidhuq'' • Change all 0s to 1s and all 1s to 0s', '["A only. A", "B only. B", "A and B only. A B", "All of the above", "None of the lsisjla 2228 is also equal to the 2228 o 0 1 0 1 0 23 22 21 20 23 22 21 20 1 x 8 0 1 x 1 0 2 0 9 9216is also equal to the 9216ixLHdjg o since B and C are the correct answer is answer number B C wxl 4 Questions 4 and 5 are based on the below circuit. m%Yak wxl 4 5 that an alarm is fixed to the Q output that when Q outputs a true value the alarm will sound and turns off when Q = 0. Q lr nj lrkak. Q kdo Q = 0 th What will be the Q output if SET is turned to 0 ?"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Correct answer: "All of the above". This follows from converting/calculating the given number(s) using standard place-value (base) arithmetic.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2197, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02198', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'Which of the following answer represents bitwise XNOR operation of the two binary numbers 110010112 and 111010102?', '["110111102", "100111102", "111000102", "101110112", "1101111"]'::jsonb, 1, 'XZ̅̅̅̅(X̅ + Z)(Y + Y̅) (X̅ + Z̅)(X̅ + Z)(Y + Y̅) De Morgan''s theory (X̅ + Z̅)(X̅ + Z)', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2198, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02199', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'In 1944, IBM along with Howard Aiken of Harvard University created the Automatic Sequence Controlled Calculator. This made it possible to solve complex math problems without an operator. What was the name of this machine? 1944 whslka iu. IBM iud.u tlaj iajhxl%Sh wkql%uk fuu.ska lrjkafkla . ku 2) Explanation', '["ABC The Atanasoff-Berry developed in the late 1930s and early was an earlier digital computer but not related to the ASCC or Mark I. 1930 w.Nd.fha 1940", "EDV AC The Electronic Discrete Variable Automatic Computer was an early binary-stored program but it was developed in the late 1940s", "IBM 704 This was one of IBM''s early introduced in 1954, but it came much later than the Mark I. 1954", "EDSAC The Electronic Delay Storage Automatic Calculator was one of the first stored -program in the late 1940s at the University of m%udo iajhxl%Sh hkq 1940 ,o ,o Mark I is the correct ud\u00bela I", "ABC"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Correct answer: "EDV AC The Electronic Discrete Variable Automatic Computer was an early binary-stored program but it was developed in the late 1940s". This follows from converting/calculating the given number(s) using standard place-value (base) arithmetic.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2199, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02200', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'Which of the following is not a valid Binary Coded Decimal (BCD) value?', '["Magnetic tapes", "Floppy disk", "SD cards", "Punch cards", "None of the above"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Correct answer: "Punch cards". This follows from converting/calculating the given number(s) using standard place-value (base) arithmetic.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2200, 'A/L Past Paper Series')
