('alict_02394', 6, 'Programming Concepts (Python/PHP)', 'Consider the below python = open(''students.txt'', ''r'') f2 = open(''grades.txt'', ''w'') line = f1.readline() while = (line.strip()).split(" ") average = (float(data[1]) + float(data[2]) + float(data[3]))', '["80.00 75.00 91.67", "240.00 225.00 275.00", "Charlie 70 80 90 Corvo 85 75 65 Blake 90 95", "Charlie 240.00 Corvo 225.00 Blake 275.00", "Charlie Corvo Blake The while (line) loop runs as long as there is a line of text to read. while (line) \u00c6mh ;dla Odjkh data = (line.strip()).split(\" \") line.strip() removes any leading or trailing spaces or newlines from the line. line.strip() spaces tlla kj f\u00beLd lrhs'' split(\" \") splits the line into parts by spaces. It separates the student''s name and their grades. split(\" \") spaces fldgia th ku Tjqkaf.a fY%aKs fjka lrhs'' For example"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Correct answer: "80.00 75.00 91.67". Trace through the code line by line (variable assignments, loop iterations, and function calls) to confirm this result.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Programming Concepts (Python/PHP)', NULL, 2394, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02395', 6, 'Programming Concepts (Python/PHP)', 'Which of the following Python expressions correctly checks if x is not equal to', '["x", "x y This syntax is used in some programming languages like but it is not valid in Python . Python does not use the operator for inequality. LKavh jeks iuyr", "x /= y An assignment operation in divides x by y and assigns the result back to x. Not a comparison operator", "x != y This is the correct way to check if x is not equal to y in Python . This comparison operator evaluates to True if the values of x and y are not and False otherwise. Python y g iudk ixikaok l%shdlre x y iudk", "x Y Not a valid syntax in Python"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Correct answer: "x". Trace through the code line by line (variable assignments, loop iterations, and function calls) to confirm this result.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Programming Concepts (Python/PHP)', NULL, 2395, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02396', 6, 'Programming Concepts (Python/PHP)', 'What is the output given by the Python statement A == ''A''?', '["True", "0", "1", "name is not defined", "False"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The Python statement A = = would indeed result in a if the variable A is not defined before the comparison. A is not The statement assumes that A is a variable that has been assigned a value', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Programming Concepts (Python/PHP)', NULL, 2396, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02397', 6, 'Programming Concepts (Python/PHP)', 'Which of the following statements about file handling modes in Python is correct?', '["The mode is used to open a file for both reading and writing. hk folgu f.dkqjla", "The mode is used to read from a and it raises an error if the file does not exist. f.dkqjla", "The mode will create a new file if it doesn''t and if the file it will overwrite its content", "The mode ''r+'' is used to open a file for writing and it creates a new file if it doesn''t exist", "The mode will append data to the end of the file if it already exists"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Option 1: Incorrect statement. The mode is used only for not both reading and writing. Option 2: Incorrect statement. The mode is for appending to a not reading. It doesn''t raise an error if the file doesn''t exist but creates a new file', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Programming Concepts (Python/PHP)', NULL, 2397, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02398', 6, 'Programming Concepts (Python/PHP)', 'How can you concatenate two str1 and str2, in Python?', '["str1 + str2", "str1.concat(str2)", "str1 & str2", "str1", "concat(str1, str2)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The correct answer is 1) str1 + str2. In strings can be concatenated using the which combines two or more strings into a single string. For if str1 contains "Hello" and str2 contains "World", the expression str1 + str2 would result in', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Programming Concepts (Python/PHP)', NULL, 2398, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02399', 6, 'Programming Concepts (Python/PHP)', 'What is the output of the below python code if the input is letter "z"?', '["z", "C", "S", "CS", "Error"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The code checks if the input letter is equal to its uppercase version using the upper() method. upper()method When the input is "z", the condition Z == letter.upper() is false because Z holds "z" (lowercase) and letter.upper() returns "Z" (uppercase)', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Programming Concepts (Python/PHP)', NULL, 2399, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02400', 6, 'Programming Concepts (Python/PHP)', 'Which of the following python statement compiles correctly?', '["x = int(''5.5'')", "print(\"Hello World\" +", "3) x = [1, 2, 3] + (4, 5,", "result = 10", "if x = 10: print(x)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Correct answer: "result = 10". Trace through the code line by line (variable assignments, loop iterations, and function calls) to confirm this result.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Programming Concepts (Python/PHP)', NULL, 2400, 'A/L Past Paper Series')
